---
const points = [
  {
    verb: 'The brand',
    body: 'The name and the visual language were <strong>shaped through an agentic process</strong>: options generated and explored by machine, <strong>the final call made by a person</strong>. A family of related identities, not a logo handed down.',
  },
  {
    verb: 'The build',
    body: 'The tools, and this very site, are developed the <strong>agent-centric way we advocate</strong>: work driven from <strong>written specs and agent sessions</strong>, each change tracing back to an intent you can read. <strong>Assistance, never autopilot.</strong>',
  },
  {
    verb: 'The design system',
    body: 'One shared, <strong>token-based design system</strong> underpins every surface. Agents maintain and migrate it across products, and an <strong>automated audit fails the build on any drift</strong>. This site\'s content is <strong>schema-validated</strong> too, so a wrong edit does not ship; it stops the build instead.',
  },
];
---

<section id="method" class="section method" data-sl-fx-backdrop>
  <div class="method__bed" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="wrap method__grid">
    <div class="method__head">
      <div class="section-index">
        <span class="section-index__no">03</span>
        <span class="eyebrow">The method</span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="h2">Made the way it's meant to run.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Sondalab builds tools for directing agents, and it is{' '}
        <strong>built by directing them</strong>. People set the intent and{' '}
        <strong>hold the switch</strong>; the machine does the work, in the open,
        every step <strong>traceable and reversible</strong>. The brand, the
        design system, and this very site came out of that same loop.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="method__points sl-fx-goo sl-fx-goo--drift">
      {points.map((p, i) => (
        <li class="mslot sl-fx-goo__member">
          <span class="mpoint__no glass-thick sl-fx-glass sl-fx-glass--pane">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <article class="mpoint glass-thick sl-fx-glass sl-fx-glass--pane sl-fx-aurora" data-curtains data-sl-fx-live="on">
            <div class="mpoint__body">
              <h3 class="mpoint__verb">{p.verb}</h3>
              <p set:html={p.body}></p>
            </div>
          </article>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* The bed, and the same plate-not-gradients rule as the stance and the tools:
     effects-gpu.js samples --slfx-backdrop as a url() and nothing else, so a
     bed painted in CSS alone is invisible to the shader. Here it matters twice
     over, because the substance above it is translucent and this is what shows
     through. scripts/make-backdrops.mjs generates it from the kit's tokens. */
  .method {
    position: relative;
    --slfx-backdrop: url('/backdrops/method-dark.svg');
  }
  :root[data-sl-theme='light'] .method {
    --slfx-backdrop: url('/backdrops/method-light.svg');
  }
  .method__bed {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: var(--slfx-backdrop) center / cover no-repeat;
  }
  .method .wrap { position: relative; z-index: 1; }

  .method__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem);
  }
  @media (min-width: 52rem) {
    .method__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 0.9fr 1.1fr;
      align-items: start;
    }
    .method__head {
      position: sticky;
      top: 6rem;
    }
  }
  .method__head .h2 { margin-top: 1.25rem; max-width: 16ch; }
  .method__head .lede { margin-top: 1.5rem; max-width: 42ch; }

  /* The three points are one substance that will not hold still. The gap is
     laid out a hair INSIDE the field's own weld, so the members start joined
     and the drift is what parts them: at 28px a 1.6rem gap welds, and an
     amplitude of 6px per member swings the distance between two of them by up
     to 12px either way — enough to cross the threshold and come back.

     The padding is what the container owes them. The substance reaches
     --slfx-goo-reach plus three blur sigmas past the outermost member, and the
     drift adds its amplitude on top; the negative inline margin gives it back,
     so the type stays aligned with the column beside it. */
  .method__points {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    --slfx-goo-weld: 28px;
    /* The kit's defaults were too quiet here, measured rather than guessed.
       --sl-bg-surface-raised against the canvas is 1.15:1 — the default suits a
       member that carries a colour of its own, and these carry none, so the
       whole slab was that grey. Lifting it toward the ink puts it near 1.7:1,
       which reads as material without becoming a card.

       6px over a 17s period is a peak speed of about 2px a second, which is
       below what anyone watches. At 12px the necks cross the 28px weld visibly
       — and it is still inside the kit's own bound of half the weld. */
    --slfx-goo-fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--sl-text-primary) 15%, var(--sl-bg-surface-raised));
    --slfx-goo-drift: 12px;
    padding: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    margin-inline: calc(-1 * clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2rem));
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.6rem;
  }

  /* The substance lets the bed through. `opacity` on the FIELD, not a
     translucent fill: goo-field.js forces --slfx-goo-fill opaque on purpose,
     because the goo chain's alpha slope erases everything below 0.5 and a
     translucent silhouette would simply not survive the filter. Opacity is
     applied to the filter's RESULT, so the weld is already computed when it
     lands and every neck stays exactly where it was.

     Safe here because no member of this field carries a fill of its own — the
     cards are glass and paint on pseudo-elements, and the numerals' blobs are
     the one exception, which is drawn as a tint on purpose. On a field of
     `sl-tag`s this would shift every colour the component chose, and I would
     not do it there. :global() because the field is the kit's element, not
     this component's. */
  .method__points :global(.sl-fx-goo__field) { opacity: 0.72; }

  /* No rule between them. A border draws a line straight across a merged mass,
     which is the defect the substance exists to replace. */
  /* One column now: the numeral is no longer a cell of this grid. The left
     padding opens up to leave it the corner it hangs in. */
  .mpoint {
    display: grid;
    padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 2.25rem);
    padding-left: clamp(4.5rem, 12vw, 9rem);
    /* The card is a pane as well as a member: the pane is what puts light
       inside it — a spot that follows the pointer and an edge that turns to
       face it — where the goo only ever says these belong together. The two
       do not fight: the kit stands the pane's hairline down on a welded
       member, because where a silhouette holds the edge a rigid ring around
       it reads as broken. */
    --slg-radius: var(--sl-radius-lg);
  }

  /* THE NUMERAL IS ITS OWN PANE, which is why it had to leave the card.
     glass-lens.js refuses a lens to any element whose parent is inside another
     glass host — `el.parentElement?.closest(".sl-glass, .sl-fx-glass")` — and
     the comment there records the investigation: the inner pane rendered
     correctly only if the ancestor gave up its lens AND its isolation
     together, and the isolation is what keeps the material under the label.
     Under that sits a browser rule the kit cannot argue with either: an
     element carrying backdrop-filter is a BACKDROP ROOT for its descendants,
     so a nested pane would refract the card's own composite and never the bed.

     So the slot is the goo member and holds two siblings. The numeral is
     absolutely positioned and out of flow, so the slot's box stays the card's
     box and the substance still welds on the card's silhouette. The slot is
     also what carries the kit's drift, which is what keeps the two registered
     while they wander — a numeral that drifted on its own clock would come
     off its card within one cycle.

     It hangs off the top-left corner on purpose: half of it is over the bed
     and half over the card, so the same figure refracts two different things
     at once. That is the one thing a pane inside the card could never do. */
  .mslot { position: relative; }
  .mpoint__no {
    position: absolute;
    left: -0.34em;
    top: -0.3em;
    z-index: 2;
    padding: 0.06em 0.1em 0.02em;
    font-family: var(--sl-font-mono);
    font-weight: 100;
    font-size: var(--step-numeral);
    line-height: 0.8;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--sl-text-primary) 72%, transparent);
    --slg-radius: var(--sl-radius-lg);
  }

  .mpoint__verb {
    font-family: var(--sl-font-display);
    font-weight: 440;
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 72, 'WONK' 1;
    font-size: var(--step-h3);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    color: var(--sl-text-primary);
  }
  .mpoint__body p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 46ch;
    color: var(--sl-text-secondary);
  }
  .method__head .lede strong,
  .mpoint__body :global(strong) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--sl-text-primary);
  }
</style>
